const P0_CREATIVE_DIRECTIONS={"tex_d079d9428d":"Acacia canopy from a harsh dry savanna: dusty olive leaf clusters, sun-bleached yellowing, copper-brown dead tips, insect damage, fine grit caught in the foliage and occasional brittle twigs. Let the plant look drought-stressed and ancient rather than lush.","tex_752fa564f0":"Dense jungle canopy after years of heat and rain: deep emerald and olive masses, bruised leaf edges, fungal speckling, damp shadow pockets, subtle vine tangles and occasional warm-decay tones. Rich and humid, but not glossy or decorative.","tex_16c5aadeaa":"Mature temperate oak foliage: layered broad leaves, mossy green undertones, slight seasonal bruising, tiny brown necrotic patches, old rain spotting and irregular shadow pockets. Make it feel like a centuries-old forest canopy.","tex_1c57c237f7":"Heavy dark-oak canopy with near-black green depth, tannin-brown veins, muted burgundy decay, lichen traces and dense old-growth shadow. It should feel oppressive, ancient and healthy enough to live, but visibly old.","tex_7af24ce86e":"Birch foliage with pale yellow-green leaves, cool undersides, wind-tattered edges, sparse brown freckles and airy gaps. Keep it lighter and more fragile than oak, like a cold forest edge battered by weather.","tex_df11566c76":"Spruce foliage as dense mats of blue-green needles with resin-dark centers, rusty dead needles, subtle frost-burn and small broken twig fragments. Coniferous, cold, severe and texturally sharp without becoming spiky noise.","tex_9419ba1c05":"Acacia trunk side with twisted charcoal-gray bark over hints of orange heartwood, drought fissures, healed scars, sun-cracked ridges, termite traces and dusty mineral staining. Make the bark feel tortured by heat and time.","tex_cba32439c1":"Jungle trunk side with rain-dark bark, deep vertical grooves, damp green-black staining, moss residue, healed vine scars and subtle fungal weathering. Dense tropical wood that has survived constant moisture.","tex_768ad40712":"Ancient oak bark with broad rough plates, deep but believable fissures, pale lichen colonies, old branch scars, moss-darkened recesses and softened rain wear. Noble, massive and long-lived rather than generic brown bark.","tex_f7a8b19fa7":"Dark-oak bark with very deep tannin-rich browns, near-black creases, old fire scorching, dry lichen crust, hard ridges and healed wounds. Give it the character of a tree that has survived storms and fire.","tex_a6cec65ac7":"Birch bark with layered ivory and ash-gray paper skin, peeling curls, black horizontal lenticels, ghostly green lichen, old scratches and weather-softened scars. Pale and eerie, not clean white.","tex_d1e3540813":"Spruce bark made of overlapping reddish-brown scales, cool gray weathering, hardened pitch stains, resin-dark cracks and small flaked plates. Rugged alpine conifer bark with visible age.","tex_b4474f8b0c":"Acacia end grain with irregular orange-copper heartwood, compressed dark growth rings, drought-stressed ring spacing, radial checks, small knot ghosts and a charred gray bark rim.","tex_bc0f92b42b":"Jungle log end grain with dense warm-brown rings, dark moisture staining, subtle fungal discoloration, tight growth history and a humid bark rim. The cut should feel freshly exposed but from very old timber.","tex_7d70c8bd91":"Oak end grain with broad readable rings, irregular growth years, radial age cracks, dark knot remnants, muted honey-brown heartwood and a rough bark boundary. Strong old timber character.","tex_0a71c1d130":"Dark-oak end grain with compressed almost-black rings, rich umber heartwood, dark mineral/tannin staining, small heart-rot traces and restrained radial splits. Dense, heavy and ancient.","tex_2e6e4057af":"Birch end grain with pale cream wood, delicate gray-beige rings, subtle spalting lines, tiny knot scars and a thin papery bark edge. Light, cold and fibrous.","tex_2850a57c42":"Spruce end grain with tight amber and pale rings, resin pockets, straight radial grain, minor drying checks and a reddish scaly bark rim. Crisp conifer growth history with cool mountain character.","tex_fe37489cde":"Acacia planks as weathered copper-orange hardwood boards with blackened knots, saw chatter, sun bleaching, dark iron/tannin streaks and repaired-looking scars. Bold warm timber, old and hard-used.","tex_2d824a5622":"Jungle planks from dense tropical hardwood: deep russet-brown grain, water staining, worm tracks, darker end-grain streaks and areas polished by age. Rich but weathered, never furniture-clean.","tex_37d73fb3d8":"Oak planks like an old medieval floor or workbench: muted honey-brown grain, broad knots, hand-tool marks, blackened nail-like staining, worn high spots and dirt in the pores.","tex_7d297eed87":"Dark-oak planks with smoked umber grain, soot-polished wear, tar or pitch streaks, hard scratches and occasional pale abrasion. Heavy, severe timber with a fortress/interior feel.","tex_f7e052c1e7":"Birch planks in pale blond and gray cream tones, faint spalting, subtle tool scars, cool dirt trapped in grain and gentle edge wear. Old pale wood, quiet and slightly ghostly.","tex_b8dabb36d1":"Spruce planks with cool tan-brown boards, resin streaks, tight knots, rough saw/axe traces, dark moisture marks and localized compression wear. Rustic alpine construction timber.","tex_a46fdac083":"Reimagine ordinary stone as an ancient compact geological surface: fine mineral clouds, subtle sediment ghosts, quartz-like flecks, pressure lines, faint fossil-like traces and weather-softened fractures. Calm overall, rewarding at close range.","tex_f1658faec1":"Cobblestone as a patchwork of old fieldstones collected over generations: varied mineral species, chipped rounded edges, packed dirt in joints, lichen freckles, tiny moss traces and stones with different histories.","tex_a73dd7c62f":"Gravel as a dense mix of broken geology: slate chips, pale quartz fragments, iron-stained pebbles, charcoal pieces, fine grit and wet-dark pockets. Natural chaotic sorting rather than a uniform pebble pattern.","tex_b49486a55b":"Sand with a distinctive mineral identity: compact fine grains, pale quartz, darker volcanic specks, rust-colored particles, tiny shell-like fragments and subtle wind sorting. Dry, old and slightly contaminated.","tex_dd1601b059":"Bedrock as primordial pressure-welded stone: black basaltic mass, fused mineral seams, obsidian-like slivers, compressed pale veins and impossible age. It should look geologically final and nearly indestructible, not simply darker stone.","tex_b0a193dba3":"Ancient glacial ice with blue-gray depth, trapped ash and dust, cloudy pressure bands, tiny suspended bubbles, thin internal fractures and dirty frozen inclusions. Cold, old and slightly ominous without magical glow.","tex_ddef61e20b":"Wind-packed old snow: off-white and blue-gray crust, granular ice crystals, faint melt-refreeze scars, soot/ash specks, tiny exposed mineral grains and subtle compacted ridges. Harsh-weather snow, not fluffy decoration.","tex_6f7e58e194":"Living dark soil: rich loam, decomposed leaf matter, fine roots, clay streaks, tiny stones, charcoal flecks, damp-dark pockets and pale dried crumbs. Fertile but old, dirty and texturally complex.","tex_366c9eb9e3":"Grass top as a rough old meadow/heath: mixed short grasses, moss, dead straw, clover-like micro plants, bare soil pinholes, bruised olive tones and uneven seasonal health. Organic diversity instead of a uniform green carpet.","tex_c20e3e8f47":"Grass side overlay as a believable hanging fringe of blades, dry straw, thin roots and creeping moss. Let some strands be long, some broken, some yellowed; add subtle soil staining while keeping the overlay's alpha structure usable.","tex_4ef3514a50":"Snowed grass side as frozen turf under a dirty compacted snow lip: icy grass tips, flattened brown blades, exposed roots, gray meltwater staining and tiny refrozen crystals. Winter damage rather than clean postcard snow.","tex_a783e34cdf":"Water source animation as a deep cold spring or still pool seen as a material surface: broad drifting caustic lace, soft dark depth, suspended silt, faint mineral tint and slow breathing ripples. Mysterious natural water, not glossy blue plastic.","tex_ad1ee4023b":"Flowing water animation with a decisive directional current: stretched ripples, shear lines, tiny turbulence seams, intermittent pale foam fragments and darker depth streaks. Let the motion feel physically driven and continuous.","tex_34fb8de84a":"River-water source with tannin-stained natural color, suspended silt, faint tea-brown/green undertones, subtle surface interference and soft sediment shadowing. Earthy river water rather than pristine fantasy blue.","tex_a516347c94":"Flowing river water with braided current lines, silvery shear, brown-green depth, sediment streaks and occasional tiny foam breakup. More energetic and dirty than the source-water version, with believable downstream motion.","tex_f977b22e14":"Lava source as a slow volcanic cauldron: drifting black crust rafts, deep orange-red molten windows, gas blisters, cooling skins, ember-like seams and mineral scum. Heavy and geological, not neon fire.","tex_1e8645ebd0":"Flowing lava as ropey viscous channels under tearing black crust: stretched hot seams, folded pahoehoe-like skin, collapsing plates, dark cooling edges and hotter undercurrents. Make the direction of flow unmistakable and physically heavy."};
